t making sure that the nhs and and  that will depend on the  capacity and the pressures in those  local areas is able to cope  but this is about bridging through to a  better place in the spring where again  subject to the regulatory approvals but  we're making good progress we'll be in a  position with the vaccine  we've already secured 355 million doses  of the vaccine we've got a new  uh nadim zaha we're really driving that  area that area of policy forward  um and and i actually think that there  is a more hopeful message  amidst of course uh the fatigue people  feel in the winter months  is it uh correct uh that the government  is thinking about breaking  the regions down into smaller areas so  for example we've seen the kind of as  you say like county-wide or  or sub-region-wide levels of tears  you think about breaking that down into  small error areas that would allow some  people to come out of  the stricter tiers earlier well we'll  have a review every two weeks and of  course there'll be i'm sure debate  this week on it but specifically on that  is that under consideration well we  always  look at all things but the problem is  the one that i mentioned before  that you've got to get the geographic  size sufficiently effective  that you don't find if you like the  smaller enclaves of lower level virus  shooting up because they're not subject  to the restrictions uh in the higher  level areas around them  um mps also want to see a cost benefit  analysis of all this so looking at the  costs of the tier system too  have you done that so first of all a  huge amount of transparency on data both  from the scientists and sage but also  from the ovr this week  what we will do in advance of the vote  uh uh this week is make sure that there  is  analysis of the cost benefits of both  the covid health outcomes the non-coded  health outcomes  economic impact and indeed the social  impact and i've been talking to  colleagues and i think this is a fair  question people 